From: Chimera <chimera@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] mfd: madera-core: add settle delay after reset release
 before first SPI register access

madera_dev_init() releases the codec's hardware RESET line and then
immediately performs the first SPI register read (MADERA_SOFTWARE_RESET,
the chip-ID check) with no delay in between. The chip's internal boot
ROM needs a short settle time after reset deassertion before its SPI
control interface reliably responds; racing that window causes the ID
read to fail, which aborts the whole probe (goto err_reset) and leaves
the codec completely unpowered until something manually re-triggers a
bind.

This is timing-sensitive and more likely to lose the race on a cold
boot (PMIC rail ramp-up and reset-release timing are less predictable
coming from fully powered-off) than on a warm reboot (rails are often
already primed from the prior cycle), which matches the cs47l93 on
star2lte intermittently failing to probe specifically on cold boot.

Add a conservative 5-10ms settle delay between releasing reset and the
first register access, matching the margin the Cirrus CS47L92/93
datasheet's power-up sequencing calls for. This makes cold boot behave
identically to warm reboot with no userspace rebind step required.

Signed-off-by: Chimera <chimera@localhost>
---
 drivers/mfd/madera-core.c | 3 +++
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/mfd/madera-core.c b/drivers/mfd/madera-core.c
index 0000000..0000000 100644
--- a/drivers/mfd/madera-core.c
+++ b/drivers/mfd/madera-core.c
@@ -678,6 +678,9 @@ int madera_dev_init(struct madera *madera)
 
 	madera_disable_hard_reset(madera);
 
+	/* Allow internal boot ROM / SPI interface to become ready */
+	usleep_range(5000, 10000);
+
 	regcache_cache_only(madera->regmap, false);
 	regcache_cache_only(madera->regmap_32bit, false);
 
